Each revision of a series has opened a thread of its own, so the
versions of one series and their review are spread across the list.
In "Re: [RFC] threading strategies for RFC and patch versions" [1],
Pat chose git.git's convention instead: a revision replies to the
first version's cover letter, or its only patch, so every version
stays in one thread.

Patch 1 makes format-patch.sh pick the reply target. It already has
v(N-1) from --previous, so it follows In-Reply-To up from there to
v1. A revision posted in a thread of its own, under the old
convention, ends the walk, so its next version joins that thread.
Patches 2 and 3 bring lab-process-mail, the mail workflow guide,
patch-triage, and freebsd-port in line. The Link to v(N-1) line
stays; readers and the superseded tag both use it.

The series needs "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised
series" [2], which adds --previous; its patch ids are in the
prerequisite-patch-id lines below. It applies to b/dev@upstream.

The superseded tag in "[PATCH v3] notmuch: Tag series a later version
replaces" [3] tags a replaced version's whole thread. Once revisions
share v1's thread, it would tag the current version too. "[PATCH v4]
notmuch: Tag series a later version replaces" [4] tags each replaced
version and its review instead. Apply it before or with this series.

I tested patch 1 against a scratch maildir. With --previous naming a
v2 or v3 that replies to v1, it threads under v1. With a revision
posted in its own thread, it threads under that revision. With an
unknown Message-ID it stops with an error. A dry run of this series'
prerequisite as a v3 threads its cover under that series' v2, which
was posted in its own thread.

